<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Colmar</h1><p class="st-meta">1226 CE – 1679 CE (approx.) · capital Colmar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alsatian wine town that was an imperial city and member of the Decapolis league until Louis XIV's France absorbed it in the 1670s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> grew from a Carolingian estate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lauch">Lauch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-alsace">Upper Alsace</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> favoured it, and it received town rights in 1226 from Frederick II, becoming an imperial city. In 1354 it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a>, the league of ten Alsatian imperial cities under the imperial bailiff at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-haguenau">Haguenau</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guilds</a> and patricians shared power in the council. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> prospered from the Alsatian wine trade and crafts, and the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-schongauer">Martin Schongauer</a> worked there in the 15th century. The city adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1575. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years War it was held by Swedish and then French troops, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a> came under French protection.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> in 1673, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1679 confirmed French rule. It was German from 1871 to 1918 and again from 1940 to 1945, and is now a French town famous for its old quarter and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isenheim-altarpiece">Isenheim Altarpiece</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1226 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.10%;width:9.01%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1226 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Colmar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alsatian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colmar, Kolmar</dd></dl></aside></div>

